==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|eschete}}, from {{der|en|xno|escheat}}, {{der|en|fro|eschet}}, {{m|fro|escheit}}, {{m|fro|escheoit||that which falls to one}}, from the past participle of {{m|fro|escheoir||to fall}}, from {{der|en|VL.|*excadō}}, from {{der|en|la|ex}} + {{m|la|cadō||I fall}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əsˈt͡ʃiːt/}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} The [[return]] of [[property]] of a [[deceased]] person to the [[state]] (originally to a [[feudal]] [[lord]]) where there are no [[legal]] [[heir]]s or [[claimant]]s.
# {{lb|en|legal}} The property so [[revert]]ed.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[plunder|Plunder]], [[booty]].
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', III.viii:
#*: Approching, with bold words and bitter threat, / Bad that same boaster, as he mote, on high / To leaue to him that Lady for '''excheat''', / Or bide him battell without further treat.
# That which falls to one; a reversion or return.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=V|stanza=25|passage=And by my ruines thinkes to make them great: / To make one great by others losse, is bad '''excheat'''}}

====Quotations====
* {{seeCites|en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put (land, property) in escheat; to [[confiscate]].
#*'''2016''', {{w|Peter H. Wilson}}, ''The Holy Roman Empire'', Penguin 2017, p. 329:
#*:Failure to perform duties opened the culprit to charges of ‘felony’ (''felonia''), providing grounds for the king to '''escheat''' the fief.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To revert to a [[state]] or [[lord]] because its previous owner died without an heir.

